            <ArticleTitle>Altered regional blood flow in the fetus: the origins of cardiovascular disease?</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1559-60</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="UNLABELLED">A fetal response to hypoxia and other adverse influences is to redistribute blood flow in order to &quot;spare&quot; the brain. This, however, is associated with reduced growth of the liver and kidneys.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The long-term consequences of fetal redistribution of blood flow are unknown, but preliminary evidence suggests that there is an increased risk of coronary heart disease and hypertension in later life.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
